<main class="library"><nav class="crumb"><a href="/en/">English home</a> / Popular library</nav><header><p class="eyebrow">OPEN READING · STUDY · HISTORY · CULTURE</p><h1>The Martín Fierro <em>Popular Library</em></h1><p class="deck">A welcoming, source-conscious place to study Argentina's gaucho epic. Start with the story, follow its historical world, or explore the words and literary debates that make the poem remarkable.</p></header><section aria-label="Published guides" class="grid">{guides.map(g=><article><a href={g.url}><img src={g.image} alt="Modern editorial illustration associated with this study guide" loading="lazy"/><div><h2>{g.title}</h2><p>{g.desc}</p><strong>Read the guide ↗</strong></div></a></article>)}</section><aside class="note"><h2>What makes this a documentary library?</h2><p>The published guides are written in English, clearly linked to source materials, and separate historical evidence from literary interpretation. The original Spanish poem remains available in our <a href="/leer/">complete reader</a>. New guides appear here only when they have real content and working destinations.</p><p><a href="/en/archive/">Historical archive</a> · <a href="/en/magazine/">Cultural magazine</a> · <a href="/en/jose-hernandez/">José Hernández</a></p></aside></main>
